00:00:02.429 --> 00:00:06.629
Film Courage Video Diary With Actor/Filmmaker/Playwright Douglas Taurel. I'm Douglas Taurel. I'm an actor and I'm
a filmmaker and I want to share a few

00:00:06.629 --> 00:00:10.980
lessons that I think every
first-time director should know and that

00:00:10.980 --> 00:00:15.020
will serve you really well on your first
project.

00:00:15.050 --> 00:00:19.550
I think the first thing you should do is
read and research read at least two

00:00:19.550 --> 00:00:24.080
books use the books as mentors there's a
level experience that you just don't

00:00:24.080 --> 00:00:28.400
have as a first-time director and by
reading a few books you get so many you

00:00:28.400 --> 00:00:33.290
get so much information on things to do
and things not to do from how to direct

00:00:33.290 --> 00:00:38.690
a scene to how not to direct a scene
what seems to never film things you

00:00:38.690 --> 00:00:43.790
should never do from positions and crew
and people on you and your crew that you

00:00:43.790 --> 00:00:50.269
must have where to save money where to
spend your money what to do with

00:00:50.269 --> 00:00:54.409
locations and those are things that you
know if you've directed multiple

00:00:54.409 --> 00:00:58.430
projects you had that experience and you
you can apply it to your project but

00:00:58.430 --> 00:01:04.129
when you've never directed a film it's a
it's a label it's a level of experiences

00:01:04.129 --> 00:01:08.750
you just don't have and by using the
books as mentors and developing and

00:01:08.750 --> 00:01:14.000
reading and getting some research out of
them you you use them as mentors and

00:01:14.000 --> 00:01:16.790
they give you confidence when you direct
a film

00:01:16.790 --> 00:01:20.329
talk to directors who have more
experience than you I was so blessed to

00:01:20.329 --> 00:01:25.399
talk to friends of mine who had directed
really successful films and had a lot

00:01:25.399 --> 00:01:28.549
more experience to me and they sit down
with me and they gave me the do's and

00:01:28.549 --> 00:01:34.340
don'ts and the lessons they gave me they
served me incredibly well when I came to

00:01:34.340 --> 00:01:38.539
set and they made me feel like an
experienced director because I was able

00:01:38.539 --> 00:01:44.749
to steal some of their experiences and
use them as a way to build myself as a

00:01:44.749 --> 00:01:49.759
new director to develop my own voice as
a director and you'll feel so much more

00:01:49.759 --> 00:01:55.490
confident when you come to set knowing
that at least you kind of have a sky

00:01:55.490 --> 00:02:04.240
post and a roadmap of things to do and
things not to do so read and research

00:02:04.290 --> 00:02:08.840
I think the next most important thing
you need to do as a first-time director

00:02:08.840 --> 00:02:15.150
is storyboard your film storyboarding
and knowing how to stage your actors is

00:02:15.150 --> 00:02:21.750
probably here a homework as a director
and storyboarding doesn't have to be

00:02:21.750 --> 00:02:26.700
really complex it could be like stick
figures and that's what I did I drew out

00:02:26.700 --> 00:02:31.110
stick figures with my film and then I
hired somebody off a Fiverr to kind of a

00:02:31.110 --> 00:02:35.850
storyboard artist to kind of you know
render them really nicely for me and you

00:02:35.850 --> 00:02:40.230
don't have to do that I do that because
I'm a little OCD and I wanted to I

00:02:40.230 --> 00:02:43.620
wanted to give something really
professional to my DP and the rest of my

00:02:43.620 --> 00:02:50.310
team but you know the most important
thing is for you to have an idea of what

00:02:50.310 --> 00:02:55.290
each shot needs to be when you write a
story you don't really see it frame by

00:02:55.290 --> 00:03:00.570
frame you kind of see it as this
polished movie in your head but in

00:03:00.570 --> 00:03:04.500
actuality when you're shooting it you're
shooting it frame by frame take bite

00:03:04.500 --> 00:03:09.780
take scene by scene it builds the film
in your head you realize you know one

00:03:09.780 --> 00:03:13.560
you have too many shots for a particular
scene

00:03:13.560 --> 00:03:17.640
you realize this is how I want to stage
my actors this is where I want to move

00:03:17.640 --> 00:03:22.860
my actors storyboarding is just critical
in developing your language and how

00:03:22.860 --> 00:03:25.890
you're going to speak to your DP are you
going to speak to the rest of your team

00:03:25.890 --> 00:03:29.610
how you're going to speak with your
actors because once you storyboard your

00:03:29.610 --> 00:03:34.500
film it keeps building your confidence
it's a first time director because you

00:03:34.500 --> 00:03:40.050
know your film but it also tells you how
to stage your your actors where you want

00:03:40.050 --> 00:03:44.810
a map you know your actors want you to
know that you know where to put them at

00:03:44.810 --> 00:03:49.110
you want to come to said saying this is
how I want to shoot the scene and this

00:03:49.110 --> 00:03:53.430
is where I want the actors to come from
and you know you want to be flexible and

00:03:53.430 --> 00:03:58.080
and and you want to be able to change
things up if things don't work but you

00:03:58.080 --> 00:04:02.970
need just a starting base and that
visual language of storyboarding your

00:04:02.970 --> 00:04:08.430
film allows you to talk to your DP on
how you want to shoot your film and he

00:04:08.430 --> 00:04:13.739
can use his expertise and give you
suggestions on camera lenses on dolly

00:04:13.739 --> 00:04:19.650
shots on simplifying a shot
it lets him know how many shots are

00:04:19.650 --> 00:04:23.490
gonna be in the scene how many shots are
gonna be in the day how is he gonna

00:04:23.490 --> 00:04:27.630
approach it it's probably one of the
most time-consuming things you can

00:04:27.630 --> 00:04:32.730
probably do but probably one of the most
valuable things you can do because you

00:04:32.730 --> 00:04:38.250
finally you take a story that's written
in words and you turn it into pictures

00:04:38.250 --> 00:04:42.360
and you need to draw those pictures you
need to create those pictures to realize

00:04:42.360 --> 00:04:46.500
how you're going to tell that story so
you get all these creative ideas when

00:04:46.500 --> 00:04:50.640
you storyboard to film that allow you to
basically communicate your story to

00:04:50.640 --> 00:04:56.970
everybody to your team into your actors
and storyboarding to me is just really

00:04:56.970 --> 00:05:01.830
important it really is your homework as
a director I think every director but

00:05:01.830 --> 00:05:06.440
every first-time director has to
storyboard their film

