00:17:37.150 --> 00:17:44.470
would suggest that you try to do that
get plenty of punch ins as many as you

00:17:44.470 --> 00:17:48.399
can shoot people's hands people's legs
shoot you know if you see something

00:17:48.399 --> 00:17:53.230
interesting and your gut tells you to
shoot it shoot it even if your whole

00:17:53.230 --> 00:17:56.679
crew says that's a dumping to shoot well
we are shooting Landing home I saw a

00:17:56.679 --> 00:18:00.760
caterpillar crawling on the floor when
we were outside in the park my character

00:18:00.760 --> 00:18:03.820
is a veteran who's struggling coming
back home I thought that was very

00:18:03.820 --> 00:18:07.779
symbolic of this caterpillar you know
crawling on the floor - what my

00:18:07.779 --> 00:18:13.269
character was going through and so I had
my DP shoot it it looks great

00:18:13.269 --> 00:18:18.190
if we had this amazing shot of this
caterpillar really tight shot of him or

00:18:18.190 --> 00:18:23.889
it or of it crawling on the floor and
we're gonna use it and it's in the

00:18:23.889 --> 00:18:28.809
trailer and it's gonna be used and in
the series and and you can't have enough

00:18:28.809 --> 00:18:34.630
punch ends just you know make sure you
get your axis playing with her hair of

00:18:34.630 --> 00:18:39.159
people's hands of hands on the wall
get as many punches as you can because

00:18:39.159 --> 00:18:41.830
when you're in the editing phase you
just don't know when you're gonna need

00:18:41.830 --> 00:18:46.090
that stuff and the last thing you want
to do is be cutting your film going man

00:18:46.090 --> 00:18:50.590
I wish I had a little bit more a couple
more reaction side shots or I wish I had

00:18:50.590 --> 00:18:54.070
a couple more punch ins I don't have any
more punch ins you know and get b-roll

00:18:54.070 --> 00:18:58.090
you know when you're going from location
location tell your DP to turn on his

00:18:58.090 --> 00:19:02.230
camera and just record as you're going
down the street you're gonna use that

00:19:02.230 --> 00:19:06.159
b-roll you can use that b-roll in 4/4
time lapses in between scenes make sure

00:19:06.159 --> 00:19:10.149
that you shoot outside when no one's
outside make sure you shoot in front of

00:19:10.149 --> 00:19:13.419
the house when no one's around the house
you know you want all this extra footage

00:19:13.419 --> 00:19:16.720
all these extra punches because these
are gonna be like the little bits that

00:19:16.720 --> 00:19:22.809
are gonna kind of fill your story out so
shoot it if you think you need it shoot

00:19:22.809 --> 00:19:27.190
it if you don't if you think you might
need it shoot it shoot as much as you

00:19:27.190 --> 00:19:32.280
possibly can
direct with kindness and what I mean by

00:19:32.280 --> 00:19:38.160
that is that be respectful of your
actors ask them what they need and try

00:19:38.160 --> 00:19:43.620
to do everything you can to relax them
on set a relaxed actor is a good actor

00:19:43.620 --> 00:19:49.230
and acting for 20 years I've been
blessed to pick things up with directors

00:19:49.230 --> 00:19:52.800
who I have a lot of respect for that I
could apply to my craft as a director

00:19:52.800 --> 00:19:57.809
and I've unfortunately I've been on sets
where directors didn't know how to

00:19:57.809 --> 00:20:03.210
direct actors and so I knew exactly what
not to do to my actors I think when you

00:20:03.210 --> 00:20:07.590
yell action it should be quiet
you should be you don't need to bark it

00:20:07.590 --> 00:20:14.309
out like some egomaniac it should just
be you know action guys because you know

00:20:14.309 --> 00:20:18.390
acting is tough but it's tough enough as
it is and and you don't need to kind of

00:20:18.390 --> 00:20:22.920
you know get people's anxieties up by
barking action at them and when you give

00:20:22.920 --> 00:20:27.540
direction give it to them privately you
know no one else needs to know what the

00:20:27.540 --> 00:20:32.490
direction is between you and your actor
I gave every note to all of my actors

00:20:32.490 --> 00:20:37.020
privately even when it was good you know
I I didn't need to let everybody else

00:20:37.020 --> 00:20:43.440
know what I was trying to communicate
with my with my actor and when you're

00:20:43.440 --> 00:20:47.429
always communicating privately to your
actors and every scene throughout the

00:20:47.429 --> 00:20:52.590
film shoot the other actors don't feel
like oh he's giving me a note now it

00:20:52.590 --> 00:20:57.480
means I'm not a good actor they realized
that this director gives notes privately

00:20:57.480 --> 00:21:02.790
after every take after every scene and
it relaxes them they know that this is

00:21:02.790 --> 00:21:07.110
the process you know when I've been on
sets when directors have just barked at

00:21:07.110 --> 00:21:10.620
me you know trying to say you know make
it it's no good it's you know make it

00:21:10.620 --> 00:21:14.340
better you know do something different
really loud and it's just a horrible way

00:21:14.340 --> 00:21:19.290
to receive direction I mean that's you
can't be any more vague and vague and I

00:21:19.290 --> 00:21:25.410
don't know what to do with that and so
just remember that if you can relax your

00:21:25.410 --> 00:21:31.200
actors they're gonna give you amazing
work and when I look at my footage when

00:21:31.200 --> 00:21:36.600
I'm cutting it I see relaxed actors I
see actors who are giving it their all

00:21:36.600 --> 00:21:43.090
and I tend to I want to think that it's
because I worked so hard to make sure

00:21:43.090 --> 00:21:49.269
they felt respected and that they felt
relaxed and so director actors with

00:21:49.269 --> 00:21:53.320
kindness your film project will
appreciate it

00:21:53.320 --> 00:21:58.660
a little trick that I learned directing
and that I learned as an actor that

00:21:58.660 --> 00:22:04.240
works really well on a film set is
improv when your actors are struggling

00:22:04.240 --> 00:22:08.260
in a scene
tell them to improv something tell them

00:22:08.260 --> 00:22:12.190
to make up a story tell them to talk
about their day tell them to talk about

00:22:12.190 --> 00:22:15.280
what they're gonna do for vacation
anything anything that's gonna loosen

00:22:15.280 --> 00:22:19.540
them up and get them more grounded and
make them feel like they're human again

00:22:19.540 --> 00:22:23.020
because sometimes you know when you're
in the scene and you're not connecting

00:22:23.020 --> 00:22:27.010
with it and you're struggling with it as
an actor you just don't you just feel

00:22:27.010 --> 00:22:30.340
really weird and and you don't feel
human anymore you feel like you're

00:22:30.340 --> 00:22:35.980
trying to create you're trying to force
something down down this to that of

00:22:35.980 --> 00:22:40.510
performance that you can't figure out
and a lot of times the way you ground an

00:22:40.510 --> 00:22:44.500
actor is just tell them hey you don't
tell me tell her what you're gonna do

00:22:44.500 --> 00:22:49.450
tomorrow on your day off or tell her
what your favorite food is what you love

00:22:49.450 --> 00:22:53.620
about it and and they start talking
about it and it's amazing how it loosens

00:22:53.620 --> 00:22:59.110
them up especially for actors who don't
have improv skill and you can get

00:22:59.110 --> 00:23:02.560
anybody to improv and make sure you film
that

00:23:02.560 --> 00:23:07.000
and you've filmed that and a lot of
times you can have them improv and film

00:23:07.000 --> 00:23:10.960
that and then have them starts to see
then the scene kind of clicks in because

00:23:10.960 --> 00:23:14.200
they got connected from the improv and
now they're connected and they're

00:23:14.200 --> 00:23:18.370
listening and they're talking to each
other like regular human beings in the

00:23:18.370 --> 00:23:23.630
scene so use improv when you feel like
you're getting stuck as a director

00:23:23.630 --> 00:23:28.280
and the last thing I want to leave you
with is you're gonna have many sleepless

00:23:28.280 --> 00:23:32.510
nights especially during pre-production
before you shoot the film definitely

00:23:32.510 --> 00:23:37.940
when you're shooting the film it's all
part of the process you're gonna get

00:23:37.940 --> 00:23:42.140
through it get it done I know that I
would wake up in the middle of the night

00:23:42.140 --> 00:23:48.260
just almost in a cold sweat freaking out
that what did I get myself into but you

00:23:48.260 --> 00:23:54.740
find a way we as creatives we always
find a way to get it done and when

00:23:54.740 --> 00:23:58.120
people let you down and you're feeling
completely stressed out and overwhelmed

00:23:58.120 --> 00:24:04.220
just be grateful that you're getting the
opportunity to feel these experiences

00:24:04.220 --> 00:24:10.880
because it's a privilege to make a film
to make a short film to make a webseries

00:24:10.880 --> 00:24:16.039
it's it's an endeavor it's expensive it
takes a lot of hard work and you just

00:24:16.039 --> 00:24:20.090
don't know when you're gonna get another
opportunity to do it and especially to

00:24:20.090 --> 00:24:28.220
direct your own film so be grateful that
you're in it and know that you're gonna

00:24:28.220 --> 00:24:35.240
get it done and when you go to bed every
night before you fall asleep just tell

00:24:35.240 --> 00:24:40.070
